 myself can run the  dose response curves and the large-scale  validation sets that we need in order to  understand and validate these assays for  hazard identification so one of the  things that we really like about these  cell types is that not only do they have  some of the not only do these narrow  micro tissues have some of the cells  that we care most about but they have  some of the function that we care most  about and this was something that was  unique to these micro brain tissues and  in fact one of the other mini brains  that we looked at I had hardly any in  vitro calcium dynamic at all but when we  put a calcium-sensitive dye and then  looked under a flipper what you can see  is that these neural micro tissues  actually have a fantastically robust and  repetitive calcium bursting signal and  and we measured six different parameters  within that calcium oscillation looking  at peak amplitude big spacing peak width  peak count along with rise time and  decay time  and what chain Hwang and Collin Choi my  lab did is they looked at combinations  of cell viability after one week as well  as compound induced effects on calcium  Oscillation up to six hours straddled  around the the c-max of over 80  different pharmaceuticals with a known  clinical adverse event and exposure  profiles and sorry this is a beautiful  video that we'll have to show you some  other time but that's not going to play  here today and so and what we found is  that these micro brains really give us a  robust and reproducible measure of a  number of different types of  neurotoxicity and so the video is behind  these beautiful still images were taken  by colin choi my lab and then what he  showed is that when you put on a seizure  ionic molecule like for immuno pyridine  that you can double the number of bursts  that you would see over a 45 second time  period so you've doubled the amount of  neuronal activity with a seizure  orogenic compound so it's obviously  something that's that we would hope  would be indicative of seizure-like  activity in vivo looking at other types  of more neurotoxicity  so here we treated micro brains with  compounds for 30 minutes had  concentration straddle straddling a  clinical c max and we measured the  calcium signal in these these cortical  micro tissues in response to MPTP  challenge MPTP is an interesting  molecule because it itself is not toxic  but it is a little fill a compound  that's known to cross the blood-brain  barrier and once it's in the brain MPTP  is metabolized by the glia into MP p  plus by monoamine oxidase B and the  glial cells MPP plus itself is toxic and  so I'm seeing a relatively short time  window is that this MPTP is being taken  up by these neural micro tissues  metabolized by the glial cells and then  it's directly altering the the number of  Peaks which you can see reflected there  in the peak count as  we're going at higher and higher  concentrations from the left of the  right along the x-axis and so this gives  us some some hope that these neural  microchips may be metabolically  competent and functionally responsive to  neuro degeneration at least in this case  due to MPTP looking at other models of  neurotoxicity so here we're looking at  Kanak acid which is a very commonly used  molecule for kindling and it's very  popular for in vivo models of epilepsy  it's a direct agonist of the glutamic  kn8 receptors and large doses of  concentrated solutions produce immediate  neuronal death by excited excited  toxicity and so what you can see here  within I believe a 30-minute time window  is that increasing concentrations of  Kanak acid create a very rapid loss of  not only peak amplitude but also the  peak count and so again reinforcing the  idea that these micro brains are both  metabolically competent and functionally  responsive to nerve degeneration by  Kanak acid but like I was mentioning at  the beginning we really can't rely on  these small sets to to validate our in  vitro assays we really need to be able  context of usin appropriate cut-offs on  very large validation datasets and so  we're currently running a large  validation dataset ourselves looking at  a number of different mechanisms of  neurotoxicity and then we won't have  time to go into it today but suffice it  to say that with a brief overview here  of the multitude of mechanisms of  neurotoxicity as well as the high level  message of our validation data set down  here is that we're very excited about  this micro brain model and and its  potential moving forward as we and  others helped to validate this model and  really begin to understand its potential  utility as a translational and a hazard  identification tool and with the last  couple of minutes i have before that the  talk is done first of all I have to  fulfill every toxicologists obligation  of having at least one quote from  Paracelsus per presentation so there you  go poison  and everything and no thing is without  poison the dosage makes it either a  poison or a remedy so that's my  contractually obligated request  fulfilled but the reason why I wanted to  focus on that today is because I think  that the age of toxicology as a  standalone discipline is is really  beginning to fade away I think that if  we want to have greater impact both in  discovery and development then we really  need to team up with qsp modelers and  quantitative systems toxicology modelers  so that we can take our mechanistic and  our Hazard identification tools and  really begin to turn them into  translational tools so and I'll give you  one example of the ways that that can be  done so if we were looking at the effect  of say in this case a kinase inhibitor  on bone marrow increasing concentrations  in a in a rat exploratory talk study  with an exploratory molecule caused  maturation arrest and in the erythroid  lineage and and some hypo cellularity in  the bone marrow and and they could send  the pathologist could tell by looking  underneath 